# Go binaries and build artifacts
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test binary built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Coverage output
*.out
coverage.out

# Vendor directory (we don't vendor). The mermaid bundle is committed verbatim
# so `go install` produces a self-contained binary with no toolchain, but it
# lives at pkg/aboard/web/lib/ — NOT vendor/, which Go treats specially and
# would try to resolve as module vendoring.
/vendor/

# goreleaser output
/dist/

# Local binary output (e.g., from `make build`). Anchored with a leading slash
# on purpose: a bare `aboard` pattern also matches any DIRECTORY named aboard,
# which silently excluded both .claude/skills/aboard/ — the whole skill — and
# pkg/aboard/ from the repo. Measured, not theoretical.
/aboard
/bin/

# The board itself. State, uploads, project recipes and every machine-local
# runtime file live under .aboard/ — the state file, the instance files clients
# discover the port through, the journal, per-tab logs and screenshots.
#
# This is a REAL project's posture and it is the one this repo follows: a board
# is a local, persistent, non-authoritative channel. Several developers on one
# repo each get their own; a committed one would mean a whole-file JSON conflict
# on every merge, over a conversation that was never theirs. The example board
# `aboard init --example` seeds is compiled into the binary at
# pkg/aboard/example/ instead, where it is a fixture rather than a log of
# somebody's afternoon.
/.aboard/

# test/shot.sh writes to .aboard/run/shots/, which /.aboard/ already covers.
# This entry is for the older path and for anything pointed at it by hand.
/.shots/

# Local cross-module development against a checkout of a dependency. Never a
# committed `replace` — that breaks `go install` for everyone else.
go.work
go.work.sum

# Editor / IDE
.vscode/
.VSCodeCounter

# Local logs / scratch
/logs/
*.db

# _output/ is scratch with its own .gitignore (it keeps the directory but
# ignores its contents), so there is deliberately no entry for it here.
